# Target C-II - the THIN uniqueness fork extends to growing phase-pure fences

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only strengthening of the THIN two-sided incidence system; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** THIN side of the 15-spaced one-sign strong-TB1 Horn upper family through `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Dependencies:** two-sided incidence purification; train-step cone theorem DT; THIN inverse-D directional capacity/dispersion  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_tb1_thin_growing_fence_uniqueness_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The two-sided incidence-purification theorem produced a growing braid and growing phase-pure train fences with one homogeneous two-bit meet/avoid type, but deliberately did not claim that a chord/train meeting is unique. The later phase-cone argument established uniqueness only against four fixed macroscopic guards.

The same cone argument applies simultaneously to the whole purified fence. After deleting only `O(1)` THIN chords near the three phase-sector boundaries and retaining one direction sector, one obtains a balanced subsystem of size
\[
\left({1\over12}-o(1)\right)\log_2\log D
\]
with an exact fork:

1. **ROW-UNIQUE:** every selected THIN chord meets every retained selected row exactly once; or
2. **FENCE-UNIQUE:** every selected THIN chord meets every selected left and right fence train at most once.

In the FENCE-UNIQUE case the already homogeneous incidence bits become exact intersection counts. Thus the whole growing braid/fence incidence system is one of four exact `0/1` rectangles, not merely four nonempty/disjointness patterns.

For any prescribed fixed `L`, the THIN directional-capacity theorem further leaves `Theta(log log D)` braid chords whose directions are pairwise more than `L/D` apart, without changing the uniqueness mode or the exact incidence type.

---

## 1. Input: a homogeneous growing braid/fence system

At the half-exponent choice of the logarithmic braid/fence theorem, the two-sided incidence purification gives sets
\[
F_D,\quad L_D,\quad R_D
\tag{GFU.1}
\]
of common size
\[
\boxed{
s_D=\left({1\over4}-o(1)\right)\log_2\log D
}
\tag{GFU.2}
\]
and bits `sigma_L,sigma_R in {0,1}` such that
\[
\chi(e,b)=\sigma_L
\quad(e\in F_D,b\in L_D),
\tag{GFU.3}
\]
\[
\chi(e,b)=\sigma_R
\quad(e\in F_D,b\in R_D).
\tag{GFU.4}
\]
Here `chi=1` means nonempty intersection of the straight THIN chord with the complete stopped-train midpoint curve.

The same system has a common selected-row family `A_D` with
\[
|A_D|=D^{1/2-o(1)}.
\tag{GFU.5}
\]
Every chord and every selected fence train crosses every row in `A_D`. The rows lie in one inverse-`D` projective phase. Each of `L_D,R_D` is separately phase-pure, with its transverse direction in one of the two column phase cones relative to the row phase. All selected fence trains are pairwise noncrossing.

Every chord in `F_D` is THIN:
\[
D-|e|<{1\over4D}.
\tag{GFU.6}
\]

---

## 2. Clear the three phase boundaries at constant cost

Let `[u_*]` be the common row transverse phase. As in the four-guard phase-cone theorem, define the ideal projective step sectors
\[
\mathcal S_-=
([u_*],[u_*]+\pi/3),
\tag{GFU.7}
\]
\[
\mathcal S_0=
([u_*]+\pi/3,[u_*]+2\pi/3),
\tag{GFU.8}
\]
\[
\mathcal S_+=
([u_*]+2\pi/3,[u_*]+\pi).
\tag{GFU.9}
\]
The row-train step cone is an `O(D^-1)` perturbation of `S_0`; a train in either column phase has step cone an `O(D^-1)` perturbation of `S_-` or `S_+`.

Choose one fixed clearance constant `L_0` larger than all phase-error coefficients. Delete every THIN chord whose projective direction lies within `L_0/D` of one of the three ideal sector boundaries.

The THIN fixed-tube capacity theorem gives a source-independent bound on the number of vertex-disjoint THIN chords in each such inverse-`D` tube. Hence only
\[
O(1)
\tag{GFU.10}
\]
chords are deleted.

Split the remaining chords by `S_-`, `S_0`, `S_+` and retain a largest class `F_D^*`. Then
\[
\boxed{
|F_D^*|
\ge {s_D-O(1)\over3}
=\left({1\over12}-o(1)\right)\log_2\log D.
}
\tag{GFU.11}
\]
Trim `L_D,R_D` if desired to the same size. Equations (GFU.3)--(GFU.4) remain valid.

---

## 3. Sector outside S0 gives ROW-UNIQUE

Suppose `F_D^*` lies in `S_-` or `S_+`. Every chord direction then lies outside the closed step cone of every retained selected row, with fixed inverse-`D` clearance after the phase perturbation.

By the line/train uniqueness lemma, a selected chord meets a selected row midpoint curve at most once. Exact selected-cut support already makes every chord meet every retained row at least once. Therefore
\[
\boxed{
|e\cap a|=1
\quad(e\in F_D^*,\ a\in A_D).
}
\tag{GFU.12}
\]
This is the ROW-UNIQUE branch, now retaining growing phase-pure train fences and the homogeneous two-bit fence-incidence type.

The row-order stabilization theorem can then be applied unchanged if a one-sided ordered-cable normal form is desired.

---

## 4. Sector S0 gives FENCE-UNIQUE on the whole growing fence

Suppose instead `F_D^*` lies in `S_0`. Every selected train in `L_D union R_D` belongs to one of the two column phases, so its train-step cone is an `O(D^-1)` perturbation of `S_-` or `S_+`.

After the boundary clearance from Section 2, every chord direction in `F_D^*` lies outside the closed step cone of every selected fence train. The line/train uniqueness lemma therefore gives
\[
\boxed{
|e\cap b|\le1
\quad(e\in F_D^*,\ b\in L_D\cup R_D).
}
\tag{GFU.13}
\]

Now combine this with the already homogeneous binary incidence. For every left train,
\[
\boxed{
|e\cap b|=\sigma_L
\quad(e\in F_D^*,\ b\in L_D),
}
\tag{GFU.14}
\]
and for every right train,
\[
\boxed{
|e\cap b|=\sigma_R
\quad(e\in F_D^*,\ b\in R_D).
}
\tag{GFU.15}
\]
Thus each of the four patterns
\[
(\sigma_L,\sigma_R)\in\{00,10,01,11\}
\tag{GFU.16}
\]
is now an exact intersection-count statement on two complete bipartite rectangles of diverging size.

After trimming both fences to `|F_D^*|`, the FENCE-UNIQUE subsystem has balanced size
\[
\boxed{
\left({1\over12}-o(1)\right)\log_2\log D
}
\tag{GFU.17}
\]
on each of its three shores, while all objects still coexist on the same `D^(1/2-o(1))` row stack.

---

## 5. Robust inverse-D direction separation is compatible with the fork

Fix any constant `L>0`. The THIN fixed-tube capacity theorem gives an eventual constant
\[
C_L=2+\left\lceil\sqrt{2+4L^2}\right\rceil
\tag{GFU.18}
\]
such that every radius-`L/D` projective direction tube contains fewer than `C_L` selected THIN matching edges.

Greedy selection inside `F_D^*` gives a subfamily `F_D^{sep}` with
\[
\boxed{
|F_D^{sep}|
\ge
\left({1\over12C_L}-o(1)\right)\log_2\log D
}
\tag{GFU.19}
\]
and
\[
d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}([e],[f])>{L\over D}
\quad(e\ne f).
\tag{GFU.20}
\]
Trimming each fence to the same cardinality preserves all conclusions. Thus either uniqueness mode may be assumed together with angular separation beyond any fixed phase-error scale.

---

## 6. Frontier effect

The previous four-guard theorem removed intersection multiplicity only for four selected trains. The entire homogeneous two-sided fence can now be put under the same uniqueness control.

The THIN branch therefore contains an unbounded subsystem of one of the following exact forms:

```text
ROW-UNIQUE:
    Theta(log log D) pairwise-crossing THIN chords;
    D^(1/2-o(1)) common rows, each met exactly once by every chord;
    Theta(log log D) phase-pure trains on each circumcenter flank;
    one homogeneous two-bit chord/fence incidence type.

FENCE-UNIQUE:
    Theta(log log D) pairwise-crossing THIN chords;
    Theta(log log D) pairwise-noncrossing phase-pure left trains;
    Theta(log log D) pairwise-noncrossing phase-pure right trains;
    every chord/train pair has exact intersection count 0 or 1;
    the count is constant on each whole flank;
    all coexist on D^(1/2-o(1)) common rows.
```

For every fixed `L`, either form retains `Theta(log log D)` chords with pairwise direction separation greater than `L/D`.

The remaining FENCE-UNIQUE task is now purely an order problem for exact pseudoline-type incidence rectangles: rule out one of `00,10,01,11` using the common row order, the two circumcenter flanks, and the fact that the chord shore is internally a pairwise-crossing matching while the train shore is internally noncrossing.

**Disposition:** upgraded the THIN phase-cone fork from four guards to the whole growing two-sided phase-pure fence, turning homogeneous meet/avoid bits into exact `0/1` intersection counts in the FENCE-UNIQUE branch. C-II and Level I remain open.
